chapter AFP

session Dual_Write_Core = Dual_Write_Layer0 +
  description "
    The Dual-Write Theorem: a self-contained study of dual writes in the
    change-data-capture (CDC) setting. A dual write sends each update to two
    independent, non-atomic stores; here the source database's committed log
    is the authority and a second downstream store is written separately.

    This is the DBLog-free core of the development. It stands on the shared
    Dual_Write_Layer0 parent (source coordinates, histories, scope, replay,
    and the virtual-cut-state predicate) rather than importing any downstream
    run or certificate machinery, so the source-coordinate type is realised by
    a conservative typedef and its order is proved, not axiomatized.

    This core entry establishes the abstract mismatch/divergence safety kernel,
    an AFP-facing admissible execution wrapper, the side-parametric
    separable/non-atomic completion theorem, the crash-closed concrete
    bad-crash trace theorem, admissibility non-vacuity checks, operational
    definition-strength negative controls, delivery/crash-truncation
    extensions, an offset-acknowledged per-store clock observation layer, a
    pairwise one-source-many-downstreams fan-out lift, a multi-key/group
    invariant divergence layer with a downstream-local crash-loss witness, and
    the DBLog-free replay/CDC positive side. Dual_Write_Execution is the main operational
    proof line; the older schedule-regression scaffold was intentionally
    dropped from this refereed session surface.

    (1) Crash divergence: the protocol kernel defines mismatch_at and divergence
    from the committed-log image. The asymmetric_store theorem is retained as an
    absence-shaped specialization of this kernel. The main execution theorem is
    side-parametric: a crash-closed implementation that admits a separable,
    non-atomic dual-write completion has a concrete crash trace with observable
    source/downstream mismatch. The later delivery, crash-truncation, and
    per-store-clock theories are additive refinements: store-clock observations
    use an independent downstream clock carrier linked to source coordinates by
    a total, functional, monotone offset-acknowledgement relation, and recover
    the older single-frontier predicates as the identity-offset special case.
    The fan-out theory keeps the same single authority and indexes downstream
    targets; each target projection is an ordinary two-store protocol, so
    fan-out divergence is exactly the existence of a divergent target
    projection, while replay/log-derived safety requires every selected target
    projection to satisfy the positive equality premise. It does not model a
    multi-target scheduler or atomic commit across targets. The multi-key
    layer then groups keys on the same source authority and proves that
    crash-prefix partial materialization can violate a cross-key invariant
    (the violation predicate requires distinct keys --- aliased keys reduce
    to the per-key case); the
    singleton case recovers the ordinary per-key mismatch theorem. It does not
    introduce autonomous multidatabase participants, voting, a coordinator, or
    an atomic-commit protocol.

    (2) Replay safety: a downstream that satisfies the replay-derived/store
    equality premise at an advertised frontier has no scoped mismatch there.
    Pure outbox/CDC segments, replay prefixes, and certified CDC tails are
    constructional ways to discharge this replay premise; log_derived exclusion
    remains as the class-level boundary theorem.

    (2') Two-sided characterization and its completeness: building on the replay
    premise, the entry closes a two-sided crash-safety characterization and shows
    observable crash-safety invariant in everything about the delivered stream
    but its scoped image. A verified non-atomic bounded-replay CDC relay
    (Dual_Write_Relay) is sufficient for crash-safety at every
    occurrence-complete frontier; coordinate advertisement alone is not a
    completeness certificate when occurrences tie. The matching image-level
    converse (Dual_Write_Converse,
    safe_iff_running_image_faithful) is necessary, so for a crash-closed,
    refining, Running-start implementation crash-safety holds iff the downstream
    image equals the committed-source-log image on scope at every reachable
    running frontier. Dual_Write_Premise_Tightness and its sibling theory
    Dual_Write_Refinement_Tightness certify that all three premises of that
    characterization are counterexample-witnessed: crash-closure by a drop-fails
    witness, Running-start by the already-crashed initial-mismatch class
    (Dual_Write_Converse), and refinement in both directions (a
    safe-yet-unfaithful and a faithful-yet-unsafe non-refining implementation).
    Two additive completeness theories then check the two natural axes of the
    delivered stream: neither the acknowledgement/durability axis
    (Dual_Write_Image_Completeness) nor the delivery-order axis
    (Dual_Write_Order_Completeness) carries a second, stronger characterization
    --- observable crash-safety is invariant in everything about the delivered
    stream but its scoped image.

    (3) Optional DBLog companion: this core entry proves the DBLog-free
    outbox/CDC and replay-safety prongs. The DBLog prong is a subordinated
    worked instance checked only in the companion session
    Dual_Write_DBLog_Instance, by citation to the DBLog_Virtual_Cuts result;
    it is not imported or re-derived here. WS4a factors the Layer-0
    virtual-cut-state predicate into Dual_Write_Layer0, so the DBLog and
    Dual-Write prongs now refer to one imported Isabelle constant while this
    core remains independent of DBLog run/certificate machinery.

    Soundness throughout means scoped-state equality at the chosen frontier:
    per-key in the core theorems, and bounded source-group invariant violation
    in the multi-key layer. It never claims general transactional atomicity,
    serializability, isolation, or a multidatabase atomic-commit protocol. The
    impossibility is a crash-durability fact, not an FLP-style asynchronous-
    consensus result. Every theorem is conditional on the premises stated in
    its statement.

    The development is sorry-free and axiom-free: it declares no axiomatization
    or consts, and its one typedecl-free type introduction is a conservative
    typedef. The protocol kernel, admissible traces, source-first and
    downstream-first completion shapes, and replay-safety classes all have
    closed-form witnesses; the admissible wrapper is a proper restriction of
    the raw trace relation, each wellformed-state conjunct has a drop-one
    negative control, and the operational definitions for observable mismatch,
    crash closure, and source-first separated completion have closed
    definition-strength controls, so no headline result is vacuously true.

